ck_042_limit_at_a_point_add_002","difficulty":"standard","question_type":"application","question":"Which function has a finite limit as $x\\to1$?","options":["$f(x)=\\dfrac{x^2+x-2}{x^2-1}$ for $x>0$, $x\\neq1$","$g(x)=\\dfrac{x^2-x+2}{x^2-1}$ for $x>0$, $x\\neq1$","$h(x)=\\dfrac{|x-1|}{x-1}$ for $x\\neq1$","$k(x)=\\sin\\dfrac{1}{x-1}$ for $x\\neq1$"],"answer_mode":"server","feedback_revision":"7e4d37b29df1147d10d141ea","time_limit":30},{"id":"year_1_1a_math_i_calculus_limit_at_a_point_1209d96b_curated20261026_001","difficulty":"basic","question_type":"conceptual","question":"Let $X=\\{1/n\\colon n=1,2,\\ldots\\}$ and let $f\\colon X\\to\\mathbb{R}$ be $f(x)=x$. Which statement about the limit as $x\\to0$ through $X$ is correct?","options":["It has limit $0$ as $x$ approaches $0$ through $X$.","It has no limit, since $X$ contains no punctured interval.","It has no limit, since $f(0)$ is not defined.","It has limit $1$, since $1$ belongs to $X$."],"answer_mode":"server","feedback_revision":"71ec213ebb878e01002fec93","time_limit":40},{"id":"year_1_1a_math_i_calculus_limit_at_a_point_1209d96b_curated20261026_002","difficulty":"basic","question_type":"conceptual","question":"If the limit of a function at a point exists, can it have two different values?","options":["If a limit exists, it is unique","Every function has a limit at every point","A limit exists only when $f(x_0)$ is defined","Two different limits may exist at the same point"],"answer_mode":"server","feedback_revision":"7eb74fcc0eaff2651d9aaf90","time_limit":25},{"id":"year_1_1a_math_i_calculus_limit_at_a_point_1209d96b_curated20261026_003","difficulty":"basic","question_type":"application","question":"Let $f\\colon\\mathbb{R}\\to\\mathbb{R}$ satisfy $\\lim_{x\\to x_0}f(x)=L$. Which condition must hold for every $\\varepsilon>0$, with each implication tested for every $x\\in\\mathbb{R}$?","options":["There is a $\\delta \\gt 0$ such that $|x-x_0| \\lt \\delta$ implies $|f(x)-L| \\lt \\varepsilon$","For every $\\delta \\gt 0$, $0 \\lt |x-x_0| \\lt \\delta$ implies $|f(x)-L| \\lt \\varepsilon$","There is a $\\delta \\gt 0$ such that $|f(x)-L| \\lt \\varepsilon$ implies $0 \\lt |x-x_0| \\lt \\delta$","There is a $\\delta \\gt 0$ such that $0 \\lt |x-x_0| \\lt \\delta$ implies $|f(x)-L| \\lt \\varepsilon$"],"answer_mode":"server","feedback_revision":"c17b598251e54624c3d62619","time_limit":40},{"id":"year_1_1a_math_i_calculus_limit_at_a_point_1209d96b_curated20261026_004","difficulty":"basic","question_type":"application","question":"A function is not defined at $x_0$, but it is defined for all nearby $x\\ne x_0$ and approaches $L$. Which statement is consistent?","options":["The limit must equal $f(x_0)$ by definition","The limit cannot exist because $f(x_0)$ is undefined","Only the one-sided limits can exist at $x_0$","The function may still have limit $L$ at $x_0$"],"answer_mode":"server","feedback_revision":"c4a7ddf515502e08f469088c","time_limit":35},{"id":"year_1_1a_math_i_calculus_limit_at_a_point_1209d96b_curated20261026_005","difficulty":"basic","question_type":"application","question":"Evaluate $\\displaystyle\\lim_{x\\to1}\\frac{x^2-1}{x-1}$ (the expression is undefined at $x=1$).","options":["$2$","$1$","$0$","The limit does not exist"],"answer_mode":"server","feedback_revision":"c4379cbc7d7fad7fd42b8a57","time_limit":20},{"id":"year_1_1a_math_i_calculus_limit_at_a_point_1209d96b_curated20261026_006","difficulty":"basic","question_type":"conceptual","question":"With angles measured in radians, what is $\\displaystyle\\lim_{x\\to0}\\frac{\\sin x}{x}$?","options":["$1$","$0$","The limit does not exist","$+\\infty$"],"answer_mode":"server","feedback_revision":"c313e39d6bff4c2c6f6fd203","time_limit":20},{"id":"expansion_pack_042_limit_at_a_point_add_curated20261026_007","difficulty":"basic","question_type":"conceptual","question":"In the definition of $\\lim_{x\\to x_0}f(x)=L$, which error is bounded by $\\varepsilon$ for sufficiently close noncentral domain inputs?","options":["$|x-L|$","$|f(x)-L|$","$|f(x_0)-L|$","$|x-x_0|$"],"answer_mode":"server","feedback_revision":"6f0190a6a93e9de45b63375a","time_limit":25},{"id":"expansion_pack_042_limit_at_a_point_add_curated20261026_008","difficulty":"standard","question_type":"conceptual","question":"Assume $\\lim_{x\\to x_0}f(x)=L$. A valid radius $\\delta_1>0$ is known for $\\varepsilon_1>0$, and $0<\\varepsilon_2<\\varepsilon_1$. What is guaranteed when choosing a valid radius for the tighter tolerance?","options":["A valid new radius can be chosen no larger than $\\delta_1$.","Every valid new radius must be strictly smaller than $\\delta_1$.","A valid new radius must always be exactly $\\varepsilon_2$.","The old radius must already work for every smaller tolerance."],"answer_mode":"server","feedback_revision":"73f3f1a254c1caebeb1a466e","time_limit":35},{"id":"expansion_pack_042_limit_at_a_point_add_curated20261026_009","difficulty":"basic","question_type":"application","question":"Does $\\displaystyle\\lim_{x\\to0}\\frac{1}{x^2}$ exist as a real number?","options":["No; the values grow beyond every bound","Yes; it equals $0$, because $x^2$ tends to $0$","No, because $1/x^2$ is not defined at $x=0$","No; the one-sided limits at $0$ have opposite signs"],"answer_mode":"server","feedback_revision":"2dd374e90ee4f7c73b60560d","time_limit":30}]}}